            {
              "id": "03d08987-6c3c-433e-9cd0-0ab44f9e62a6",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Chess Story</strong></em> (also published as <em>The Royal Game </em>and <em>Chess: A Novel</em>) was written by Stefan Zweig, originally published in 1943."
            },
            {
              "id": "de6c3bae-b598-4f31-a1ad-28d8d33212cd",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "09e0efaa-89a4-4f4b-8cb8-79255639e149",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>'... a human being, an intellectual human being who constantly bends the entire force of his mind on the ridiculous task of forcing a wooden king into the corner of a wooden board, and does it without going mad!' A group of passengers on a cruise ship challenge the world chess champion to a match. At first, they crumble, until they are helped by whispered advice from a stranger in the crowd - a man who will risk everything to win. Stefan Zweig's acclaimed novella </em>Chess<em> is a disturbing, intensely dramatic depiction of obsession and the price of genius.</em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "641f2f34-4d2f-41c1-b95d-ec9b68352996",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
          ]
        },
